@* MobileUserDetail.razor — mobile version of the UserDetail (staff user
   detail) page.
   Route: /m/Admin/Users/{UserId:guid}
   Layout: MobileLayout

   PORTS a mobile-friendly subset of the desktop UserDetail.razor
   (2568 lines). The desktop page is a hybrid view + staff management
   surface (Edit FullName modal, Assign/Remove role dropdown, Change
   group inline panel, Remove from group, Reset password modal). MOBILE
   deliberately drops ALL multi-step management affordances — they belong
   on the desktop /m/Admin/Users surface where there's room for modals +
   dropdowns + 2-column group-card grids. What mobile keeps:

     - Page header with back button + user's full name + worker-id
       subtitle (monospace digits via CultureFormat.ToCultureDigits).
     - Profile summary card (read-only rows):
         · WorkerId
         · FullName
         · Gender (Loc[GetGenderLabel(...)] lookup — same pattern as
           MobileSettings.razor)
         · Email (only when non-empty — the DTO's Email is currently
           always null per the desktop's TODO note, but the row is
           wired to light up automatically when the handler is extended)
         · Status badge (.m-badge-success when active, .m-badge-danger
           when inactive — same color rule as MobileSettings/MobileAdminUsers)
         · GroupName chip (only when non-null — mirrors desktop's
           "show group row when GroupName is set" rule)
         · Roles chips (one .m-badge per role, color-coded by the
           desktop's GetRoleBadgeStyle mapping: Admin → danger,
           Manager → warning, Employee → info, ReadOnly → neutral,
           Customer → success)
     - Action buttons card (only when CanActivate() returns true —
       matches the desktop's role-scope rule):
         · Activate (when user is inactive — non-destructive restore,
           no confirm)
         · Deactivate (when user is active — destructive, opens an
           inline confirm with "Are you sure?" + Confirm/Cancel
           buttons, mirrors MobileCart's _confirmingClear pattern)
       Per-action _busyAction string state machine (mirrors
       MobileAdminCategories's _busyKey): "action:activate" or
       "action:deactivate" — the finally block only clears the flag
       when it still matches this action's key (prevents stale resets
       from a concurrent action).
     - Empty state (UserNotFound) when the handler returns Result.Failure
       (covers both genuinely-missing AND not-allowed-to-see — the
       handler uses a "never leak existence" pattern, same as
       SaleDetail / ProductDetail).
     - Footer with Loc["Version"].

   BACKEND CONTRACT (mirrors desktop UserDetail.razor):
     - Load: GetUserByIdQuery { UserId } → Result<UserDto>.
       [RequireAuthentication] + viewer-scope rules:
         · Admin / Manager: may view any user, including GroupName.
         · Employee: may view any user, but GroupName is hidden.
         · Customer / ReadOnly: may view ONLY their own profile
           (the handler returns Result.Failure for other users with
           a generic "not found" message — defense-in-depth against
           existence leaking).
     - Activate: ActivateUserCommand(UserId) → Result.
       [RequireRoles(Manager, Admin)]. Handler rejects self-activation
       as defense-in-depth (the UI also hides the button via CanActivate).
     - Deactivate: DeactivateUserCommand(UserId) → Result.
       [RequireRoles(Manager, Admin)]. Handler rejects self-deactivation
       (the UI also hides the button via CanActivate).

   ROLE-RESOLUTION PATTERN (matches desktop UserDetail.razor +
   MobileAdminUsers.razor):
     _isAdmin, _isManager, _isEmployee, _currentUserId are computed
     in OnInitializedAsync via AuthStateProvider.GetAuthenticationStateAsync()
     — NOT via ICurrentUserService.IsInRole (which returns false for
     everyone in interactive Blazor Server mode because HttpContext is
     null over the SignalR circuit). Same pattern as the desktop
     UserDetail.razor + MobileAdminUsers.razor + MobileSaleDetail.razor.
     The Wolverine command handlers ALSO enforce [RequireRoles] server-
     side as defense-in-depth — the UI's CanActivate is a UX gate, not
     a security boundary.

   ROLE-SCOPE RULE (CanActivate, mirrors desktop verbatim):
     - Admin    → anyone except self (handler also rejects self).
     - Manager  → target must be Employee or Customer, AND must NOT be
                  self, Manager, or Admin.
     - Employee → cannot activate/deactivate anyone (the command is
                  [RequireRoles(Manager, Admin)] — Employees are rejected
                  by the auth middleware before the handler runs).

   NOT PORTED TO MOBILE (per task spec — these belong on desktop):
     - Edit FullName modal (UpdateUserFullNameCommand).
     - Assign/Remove role (AssignUserRoleCommand / RemoveUserRoleCommand)
       — the roles list renders as READ-ONLY chips.
     - Change group / Assign to group (AssignUserToGroupCommand) — the
       group renders as a READ-ONLY chip.
     - Remove from group (RemoveUserFromGroupCommand).
     - Reset password (ResetUserPasswordCommand).

   ROUTE PARAMETER:
     `UserId` is a Guid bound from the URL via [Parameter]. The @page
     constraint `{UserId:guid}` validates the URL segment is parseable
     as a Guid before this component even loads — a malformed URL gets a
     404 from the router, not a runtime exception here. Same `:guid`
     constraint as MobileSaleDetail's `{SaleId:guid}` and
     MobileProductDetail's `{ProductId:guid}`. The route param name is
     `UserId` (NOT `Id` like the desktop uses) to mirror the underlying
     GetUserByIdQuery field name for symmetry with MobileSaleDetail's
     `SaleId` / MobileProductDetail's `ProductId` naming convention.

   ANTIFORGERY:
     Not needed — no EditForm. All buttons are regular <button>
     elements with async @onclick handlers that dispatch commands via
     Wolverine. The Blazor circuit's anti-forgery token is established
     at circuit start. No form POST happens. (Same as desktop
     UserDetail.razor + MobileSaleDetail.razor.)
*@

@page "/m/Admin/Users/{UserId:guid}"
@attribute [Authorize(Roles = "Admin,Manager,Employee")]
@layout TakOne.WebUI.Components.Layout.MobileLayout.MobileLayout

@using System.Globalization
@using System.Security.Claims
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Authorization
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Localization
@using Wolverine
@using TakOne.Application.Common.Authorization
@using TakOne.Application.Users.DTOs
@using TakOne.Application.Users.Queries.GetUserById
@using TakOne.Application.Users.Commands.ActivateUser
@using TakOne.Application.Users.Commands.DeactivateUser
@using TakOne.Domain.Users
@using TakOne.SharedKernel.Common
@using TakOne.WebUI.Services

@inject IMessageBus MessageBus
@inject NavigationManager Navigation
@inject IStringLocalizer<MobileUserDetail> Loc
@inject ToastService Toast
@inject AuthenticationStateProvider AuthStateProvider

<PageTitle>@(_user is null ? Loc["PageTitle"] : $"{_user.FullName} — {Loc["PageTitle"]}")</PageTitle>

@if (_isLoading)
{
    @* Skeleton: back-header + profile summary card (4 rows) + roles chips
       + action buttons card. Mirrors real UserDetail layout. *@
    <div class="m-page-header-back">
        <div class="m-skeleton" style="width: 2.5rem; height: 2.5rem; border-radius: 0.5rem;"></div>
        <div class="m-page-header-text" style="flex: 1;">
            <div class="m-skeleton m-skeleton-line medium"></div>
            <div class="m-skeleton m-skeleton-line short" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"></div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="m-summary-card">
        <div class="m-skeleton m-skeleton-line medium" style="width: 6rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;"></div>
        @for (var i = 0; i < 4; i++)
        {
            <div class="m-profile-row">
                <div class="m-skeleton m-skeleton-line short"></div>
                <div class="m-skeleton m-skeleton-line medium"></div>
            </div>
        }
    </div>
    <div class="m-summary-card">
        <div class="m-skeleton m-skeleton-line short" style="width: 5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;"></div>
        <div class="m-profile-row m-profile-row-stack">
            <div class="m-profile-chips">
                @for (var i = 0; i < 2; i++)
                {
                    <div class="m-skeleton" style="width: 4rem; height: 1.5rem; border-radius: 0.5rem;"></div>
                }
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="m-action-stack">
        <div class="m-skeleton" style="width: 100%; height: 2.75rem; border-radius: 0.5rem;"></div>
        <div class="m-skeleton" style="width: 100%; height: 2.75rem; border-radius: 0.5rem;"></div>
    </div>
    <span class="sr-only">@Loc["Loading"]</span>
}
else if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(_loadError))
{
    <div class="m-error-box">
        <p>@_loadError</p>
        <button class="m-btn m-btn-secondary" @onclick="LoadAsync">@Loc["RetryButton"]</button>
    </div>
}
else if (_user is null)
{
    @* Not-found / not-allowed: the handler returns Result.Failure with a
       generic "User '...' was not found." message for both genuinely-missing
       users AND viewers-not-allowed-to-see (defense-in-depth — never leak
       that the user exists). Either way the user sees the same friendly
       message + a Back-to-users button. Same pattern as MobileSaleDetail
       and MobileProductDetail. *@
    <div class="m-empty">
        <p>@Loc["UserNotFound"]</p>
        <button class="m-btn m-btn-secondary" @onclick="GoToUsers">@Loc["BackToUsers"]</button>
    </div>
}
else
{
    @* ── 1. Page header — back button + user's full name + worker-id
           subtitle. The .m-page-header-back wrapper + .m-back-btn +
           .m-page-header-text classes are mobile-detail-specific (also
           used by MobileSaleDetail + MobileProductDetail) — the
           orchestrator adds them to mobile.css in a later pass (until
           then the page renders unstyled-but-functional). *@
    <div class="m-page-header-back">
        <button type="button"
                class="m-back-btn"
                aria-label="@Loc["BackToUsers"]"
                title="@Loc["BackToUsers"]"
                @onclick="GoToUsers">
            <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="2" style="width: 1.25rem; height: 1.25rem;">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M15 19l-7-7 7-7" />
            </svg>
        </button>
        <div class="m-page-header-text">
            <h1 class="m-page-title">@_user.FullName</h1>
            <p class="m-page-subtitle">@CultureFormat.ToCultureDigits(_user.WorkerId)</p>
        </div>
        <span class="m-badge @(_user.IsActive ? "m-badge-success" : "m-badge-danger")">
            @(_user.IsActive ? Loc["StatusActive"] : Loc["StatusInactive"])
        </span>
    </div>

    @* ── 2. Profile summary card — read-only identity fields. Mirrors
           MobileSettings.razor's .m-summary-card + .m-profile-row
           pattern. The status badge, gender label, and group chip rules
           all mirror the desktop UserDetail.razor's profile-card
           rendering (just laid out vertically for mobile). *@
    <div class="m-summary-card">
        <div class="m-card-section-title">@Loc["ProfileCardTitle"]</div>

        <div class="m-profile-row">
            <span class="m-profile-label">@Loc["ColWorkerId"]</span>
            <span class="m-profile-value">@CultureFormat.ToCultureDigits(_user.WorkerId)</span>
        </div>

        <div class="m-profile-row">
            <span class="m-profile-label">@Loc["ColFullName"]</span>
            <span class="m-profile-value">@_user.FullName</span>
        </div>

        <div class="m-profile-row">
            <span class="m-profile-label">@Loc["ColGender"]</span>
            <span class="m-profile-value">@Loc[GetGenderLabel(_user.Gender)]</span>
        </div>

        @* Email row — only rendered when the handler returned a non-empty
           Email (currently always null per the desktop's TODO note, but
           the row is wired so it lights up automatically when the handler
           is extended to populate it — same guard as MobileSettings.razor. *@
        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_user.Email))
        {
            <div class="m-profile-row">
                <span class="m-profile-label">@Loc["ColEmail"]</span>
                <span class="m-profile-value">@_user.Email</span>
            </div>
        }

        <div class="m-profile-row">
            <span class="m-profile-label">@Loc["ColStatus"]</span>
            <span class="m-profile-value">
                @if (_user.IsActive)
                {
                    <span class="m-badge m-badge-success">@Loc["StatusActive"]</span>
                }
                else
                {
                    <span class="m-badge m-badge-danger">@Loc["StatusInactive"]</span>
                }
            </span>
        </div>

        @* Group row — only rendered when GroupName is non-empty. The
           desktop's GetUserByIdQueryHandler clears GroupName for
           Employee viewers + for Customer/ReadOnly self-viewers, so this
           row gracefully hides for those scopes. GroupName is populated
           for Admin/Manager viewers regardless of whether the user is
           staff or customer — same rule as the desktop's profile card. *@
        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_user.GroupName))
        {
            <div class="m-profile-row">
                <span class="m-profile-label">@Loc["ColGroup"]</span>
                <span class="m-profile-value">
                    <span class="m-badge m-badge-neutral">@_user.GroupName</span>
                </span>
            </div>
        }

        @* Roles row — read-only chip list. One .m-badge per role, color-
           coded by the desktop's GetRoleBadgeStyle mapping:
             Admin → danger, Manager → warning, Employee → info,
             ReadOnly → neutral, Customer → success.
           Role management (Assign/Remove) is intentionally dropped on
           mobile per the task spec — admins use the desktop /Admin/Users
           surface for that flow. The empty-roles fallback shows the
           desktop's NoRoles message so the row doesn't read as a UI bug. *@
        <div class="m-profile-row m-profile-row-stack">
            <span class="m-profile-label">@Loc["RolesCardTitle"]</span>
            <span class="m-profile-value m-profile-chips">
                @if (_user.Roles.Count == 0)
                {
                    <span class="m-profile-muted">@Loc["NoRoles"]</span>
                }
                else
                {
                    @foreach (var role in _user.Roles)
                    {
                        <span class="m-badge @GetRoleBadgeClass(role)">@GetRoleLabel(role)</span>
                    }
                }
            </span>
        </div>
    </div>

    @* ── 3. Action buttons card — only rendered when CanActivate()
           returns true for the current viewer + target. Matches the
           desktop's role-scope rule (Admin → anyone but self; Manager →
           Employee/Customer targets only, not self/managers/admins;
           Employee → cannot activate/deactivate anyone — the card is
           hidden entirely).

           Per-action _busyAction string state machine (mirrors
           MobileAdminCategories's _busyKey pattern): "action:activate"
           or "action:deactivate". The finally block only clears the
           flag when it still matches this action's key — prevents stale
           resets from a concurrent action. Disabled buttons render a
           small spinner to acknowledge the click. *@
    @if (CanActivate())
    {
        <div class="m-summary-card">
            <div class="m-card-section-title">@Loc["ActionsCardTitle"]</div>

            @if (_user.IsActive)
            {
                @* Active user → Deactivate (destructive, requires
                   confirmation). Inline confirm flow mirrors
                   MobileCart's _confirmingClear pattern: clicking the
                   Deactivate button opens an inline panel with the
                   confirm prompt + Confirm/Cancel buttons. No 3-second
                   countdown like the desktop's DeactivateUserDialog —
                   mobile keeps the friction minimal but the confirm
                   step prevents reflex clicks. *@
                @if (_confirmingDeactivate)
                {
                    <div class="m-inline-confirm">
                        <p class="m-inline-confirm-text">
                            @string.Format(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture, Loc["ConfirmDeactivateFormat"], _user.FullName)
                        </p>
                        <div class="m-inline-confirm-actions">
                            <button type="button"
                                    class="m-btn m-btn-secondary m-btn-sm"
                                    disabled="@(_busyAction == "action:deactivate")"
                                    @onclick="CancelDeactivate">
                                @Loc["ConfirmCancel"]
                            </button>
                            <button type="button"
                                    class="m-btn m-btn-danger m-btn-sm"
                                    disabled="@(_busyAction is not null)"
                                    @onclick="HandleDeactivateAsync">
                                @if (_busyAction == "action:deactivate")
                                {
                                    <RadzenProgressBarCircular ShowValue="false" Mode="ProgressBarMode.Indeterminate" Size="ProgressBarCircularSize.Small" />
                                }
                                else
                                {
                                    <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="2.5" style="width: 1rem; height: 1rem;">
                                        <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4.5 12.75l6 6 9-13.5" />
                                    </svg>
                                }
                                @Loc["ConfirmDeactivate"]
                            </button>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                }
                else
                {
                    <button type="button"
                            class="m-btn m-btn-danger m-btn-block"
                            disabled="@(_busyAction is not null)"
                            @onclick="StartDeactivateConfirm">
                        @if (_busyAction == "action:deactivate")
                        {
                            <RadzenProgressBarCircular ShowValue="false" Mode="ProgressBarMode.Indeterminate" Size="ProgressBarCircularSize.Small" />
                        }
                        else
                        {
                            <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="2" style="width: 1.25rem; height: 1.25rem;">
                                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M18.364 18.364A9 9 0 005.636 5.636m12.728 12.728A9 9 0 015.636 18.364m12.728-12.728L5.636 18.364" />
                            </svg>
                        }
                        @Loc["ActionDeactivate"]
                    </button>
                }
            }
            else
            {
                @* Inactive user → Activate (non-destructive, no
                   confirmation — mirrors the desktop's
                   HandleActivateAsync and MobileAdminUsers's
                   HandleActivateAsync). *@
                <button type="button"
                        class="m-btn m-btn-primary m-btn-block"
                        disabled="@(_busyAction is not null)"
                        @onclick="HandleActivateAsync">
                    @if (_busyAction == "action:activate")
                    {
                        <RadzenProgressBarCircular ShowValue="false" Mode="ProgressBarMode.Indeterminate" Size="ProgressBarCircularSize.Small" />
                    }
                    else
                    {
                        <svg fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke-width="2.5" style="width: 1.25rem; height: 1.25rem;">
                            <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4.5 12.75l6 6 9-13.5" />
                        </svg>
                    }
                    @Loc["ActionActivate"]
                </button>
            }
        </div>
    }

    @* ── 4. Footer — version string. Same .m-footer pattern as the other
           mobile pages. *@
    <div class="m-footer">
        @Loc["Version"]
    </div>
}

@code {
    // ── Route parameter ────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// User ID from the route (/m/Admin/Users/{UserId:guid}). Bound by the
    /// Blazor router — a malformed Guid segment triggers the router's 404
    /// template before this component loads. The desktop page uses `Id`
    /// as the param name — mobile mirrors the underlying GetUserByIdQuery
    /// field name (`UserId`) for symmetry with MobileSaleDetail's `SaleId`
    /// and MobileProductDetail's `ProductId` naming convention.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Guid UserId { get; set; }

    // ── State ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// The loaded UserDto (Id, WorkerId, FullName, Gender, GroupId?,
    /// GroupName?, IsActive, Email?, Roles list). Null when the load
    /// failed OR when the handler returned Result.Failure (covers both
    /// genuinely-missing AND viewer-not-allowed — the markup's not-found
    /// branch handles both cases with the same friendly message).
    /// </summary>
    private UserDto? _user;

    private bool _isLoading = true;
    private string _loadError = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// Per-action in-flight guard (mirrors MobileAdminCategories's _busyKey
    /// pattern). Set to "action:activate" or "action:deactivate" while an
    /// action is in flight; null when idle. The finally block only clears
    /// the flag when it still matches this action's key — prevents a stale
    /// reset if a concurrent action somehow interleaves (defense-in-depth
    /// against the rare race where the user double-taps before the first
    /// handler's await completes).
    /// </summary>
    private string? _busyAction;

    /// <summary>
    /// True when the inline Deactivate confirmation panel is open. Toggled
    /// by the Deactivate button (open) and the Cancel button / a successful
    /// dispatch (close). Mirrors MobileCart's _confirmingClear pattern —
    /// mobile-friendly alternative to the desktop's 3-second-countdown
    /// DeactivateUserDialog (which is too heavy for a single-tap UX).
    /// </summary>
    private bool _confirmingDeactivate;

    // ── Current-user context (mirrors desktop UserDetail.razor +
    // MobileAdminUsers.razor) ──────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// True if the current user is in the Admin role. Computed via
    /// AuthenticationStateProvider in OnInitializedAsync — NOT via
    /// ICurrentUserService.IsInRole (which returns false over the
    /// SignalR circuit because HttpContext is null). Gates the
    /// Activate/Deactivate UI's CanActivate rule. The Wolverine
    /// handlers ALSO enforce [RequireRoles(Manager, Admin)] server-side
    /// as defense-in-depth.
    /// </summary>
    private bool _isAdmin;

    /// <summary>
    /// True if the current user is in the Manager role. Combined with
    /// _isAdmin to gate the Activate/Deactivate scope (Managers can
    /// activate/deactivate Employee or Customer targets, but NOT self,
    /// other Managers, Admins, or ReadOnlys).
    /// </summary>
    private bool _isManager;

    /// <summary>
    /// True if the current user is in the Employee role (and NOT also
    /// Admin or Manager). Used by CanActivate to deny the
    /// Activate/Deactivate UI entirely for Employee viewers (the
    /// command is [RequireRoles(Manager, Admin)] — Employees are
    /// rejected by the auth middleware before the handler runs).
    /// </summary>
    private bool _isEmployee;

    /// <summary>
    /// The current user's Domain User Id (from the "UserId" claim set
    /// by our custom IClaimsPrincipalFactory in Infrastructure). Used
    /// by CanActivate to hide the Activate/Deactivate buttons on the
    /// viewer's own page (handler also rejects self-action as
    /// defense-in-depth).
    /// </summary>
    private Guid _currentUserId;

    // ── Lifecycle ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Compute _isAdmin, _isManager, _isEmployee, _currentUserId
        // synchronously via AuthStateProvider so the action-card
        // visibility doesn't flicker on first render. Same pattern as
        // desktop UserDetail.razor + MobileAdminUsers.razor.
        try
        {
            await ResolveCurrentUserAsync();
        }
        catch
        {
            // Non-fatal — _currentUserId stays Guid.Empty (no self-action
            // gate, which is safe because the handler also rejects self-
            // activation) and _isAdmin/_isManager/_isEmployee stay false
            // (the action card stays hidden — safer fallback if role
            // resolution fails). The handler still enforces [RequireRoles]
            // server-side.
        }

        try
        {
            await LoadAsync();
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Defense-in-depth — LoadAsync swallows its own exceptions
            // and surfaces a friendly _loadError, but if something
            // unexpected happens (e.g. AuthStateProvider throws during
            // role resolution), show the generic error instead of
            // crashing the circuit.
            _loadError = Loc["Error_Unexpected"];
        }
        finally
        {
            _isLoading = false;
        }
    }

    // ── Auth / role resolution ─────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Resolves the current user's Id + role flags from
    /// AuthenticationStateProvider. The UserId claim is set by our
    /// custom IClaimsPrincipalFactory in the Infrastructure layer
    /// (it's the Domain User's Guid Id); fallback to
    /// ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier for safety. Same pattern as
    /// desktop UserDetail.razor + MobileAdminUsers.razor.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ResolveCurrentUserAsync()
    {
        var authState = await AuthStateProvider.GetAuthenticationStateAsync();
        var user = authState.User;

        if (user?.Identity?.IsAuthenticated != true) return;

        var idClaim = user.FindFirst("UserId")?.Value
                      ?? user.FindFirst(ClaimTypes.NameIdentifier)?.Value;
        if (Guid.TryParse(idClaim, out var parsedId))
        {
            _currentUserId = parsedId;
        }

        _isAdmin = user.IsInRole(Roles.Admin);
        _isManager = user.IsInRole(Roles.Manager);
        // Employee-only means "in Employee role AND not also Admin or
        // Manager". Admins and Managers who happen to also be in the
        // Employee role are treated as Admin/Manager respectively
        // (broader scope). Same logic as desktop UserDetail.razor's
        // _isEmployee field + MobileAdminUsers.razor's _isEmployeeOnly.
        _isEmployee = !_isAdmin && !_isManager && user.IsInRole(Roles.Employee);
    }

    // ── Data loading ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Loads the UserDto via GetUserByIdQuery. On success, sets _user +
    /// clears _loadError. On failure (handler returned Result.Failure OR
    /// the dispatch threw), sets _loadError and clears _user (the markup's
    /// not-found branch handles both cases — the handler returns a
    /// generic "not found" for both genuinely-missing AND not-allowed-to-
    /// see, so we surface the friendly UserNotFound message instead of
    /// leaking the existence check).
    ///
    /// Also called by the Retry button in the error branch + by the
    /// post-action ReloadUserSilentlyAsync helper.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task LoadAsync()
    {
        _loadError = string.Empty;
        try
        {
            var query = new GetUserByIdQuery { UserId = UserId };
            var result = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<Result<UserDto>>(query);

            if (result.IsSuccess)
            {
                _user = result.Value;
            }
            else
            {
                // result.Error contains the handler's user-friendly message
                // (e.g. "User '...' was not found."). For the not-found
                // case, the markup's _user-is-null branch shows the friendly
                // Loc["UserNotFound"] message — we don't surface the raw
                // handler error because it could leak which (missing vs
                // not-allowed) case occurred.
                _user = null;
                _loadError = string.Empty;
            }
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            _user = null;
            _loadError = Loc["Error_FailedToLoad"];
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Silent-reload variant used after a successful Activate/Deactivate.
    /// Skips toggling _isLoading so the page doesn't flash the full-page
    /// loading spinner between the success toast and the refreshed content.
    /// Mirrors desktop UserDetail.razor's ReloadUserSilentlyAsync +
    /// MobileSaleDetail.razor's ReloadSaleSilentlyAsync pattern. Failures
    /// are non-fatal — the action already succeeded and we already toasted
    /// success; the next user interaction (or manual refresh) will surface
    /// any persistent load failure via the regular LoadAsync path.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task ReloadUserSilentlyAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            var query = new GetUserByIdQuery { UserId = UserId };
            var result = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<Result<UserDto>>(query);
            if (result.IsSuccess)
            {
                _user = result.Value;
            }
            // else: silent — keep the stale _user (the action did succeed).
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            // Silent — see remark above.
        }
    }

    // ── Role-scope rule (mirrors desktop UserDetail.razor's CanActivate) ─

    /// <summary>
    /// Returns true if the current viewer may activate/deactivate the user
    /// currently loaded on this page. Mirrors the server-side scope rules
    /// enforced by ActivateUserCommandHandler / DeactivateUserCommandHandler.
    ///
    /// Rules (per the desktop's Phase 6.6 spec):
    ///   - Admin    → anyone EXCEPT self (handler also rejects self).
    ///   - Manager  → target must be Employee or Customer, AND must NOT
    ///                be self, Manager, or Admin.
    ///   - Employee → cannot activate/deactivate anyone (the command is
    ///                [RequireRoles(Manager, Admin)] — Employees are
    ///                rejected by the auth middleware before the handler
    ///                even runs).
    ///
    /// Returns false for _isViewingSelf regardless of role (the handler
    /// rejects self-activation with "You cannot activate yourself. You
    /// are already active." — we hide the button for better UX).
    /// </summary>
    private bool CanActivate()
    {
        if (_user is null) return false;

        // Self-check — viewer is looking at their own page.
        if (_currentUserId == _user.Id) return false;

        // Admins can activate/deactivate anyone except self (already
        // checked above).
        if (_isAdmin) return true;

        // Managers can activate/deactivate Employee or Customer targets,
        // but NOT other managers, admins, or read-onlys.
        if (_isManager && !_isAdmin)
        {
            var targetIsManager = _user.Roles.Contains(Roles.Manager);
            var targetIsAdmin = _user.Roles.Contains(Roles.Admin);
            var targetIsEmployeeOrCustomer = _user.Roles.Contains(Roles.Employee)
                                          || _user.Roles.Contains(Roles.Customer);
            return !targetIsManager && !targetIsAdmin && targetIsEmployeeOrCustomer;
        }

        // Employees (and anyone else) — cannot activate/deactivate anyone.
        return false;
    }

    // ── Action handlers ────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Opens the inline Deactivate confirmation panel. No dispatch yet —
    /// the actual DeactivateUserCommand is dispatched by HandleDeactivateAsync
    /// after the user taps Confirm. Mirrors MobileCart's _confirmingClear
    /// pattern: a single inline panel with the confirm prompt + Confirm/
    /// Cancel buttons (mobile-friendly alternative to the desktop's 3-second
    /// countdown DeactivateUserDialog).
    /// </summary>
    private void StartDeactivateConfirm()
    {
        if (_busyAction is not null) return;
        if (!CanActivate() || _user is null || !_user.IsActive) return;
        _confirmingDeactivate = true;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Closes the inline Deactivate confirmation panel without dispatching.
    /// Called by the Cancel button. Silent — no toast (the user explicitly
    /// chose to abort).
    /// </summary>
    private void CancelDeactivate()
    {
        if (_busyAction == "action:deactivate") return; // don't allow cancel mid-dispatch
        _confirmingDeactivate = false;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Dispatches DeactivateUserCommand. Called by the Confirm button in
    /// the inline confirmation panel. Sets _busyAction = "action:deactivate"
    /// (mirrors MobileAdminCategories's _busyKey pattern — the finally block
    /// only clears the flag when it still matches this action's key, which
    /// prevents stale resets from a concurrent action). On success: toast
    /// + close the confirm panel + ReloadUserSilentlyAsync so the status
    /// badge flips to "Inactive" without a full-page reload. On failure:
    /// toast the handler's error (or the generic Error_Generic fallback).
    /// </summary>
    private async Task HandleDeactivateAsync()
    {
        if (_busyAction is not null) return;
        if (_user is null || !CanActivate() || !_user.IsActive) return;

        var actionKey = "action:deactivate";
        _busyAction = actionKey;

        try
        {
            var result = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<Result>(
                new DeactivateUserCommand(_user.Id));

            if (result.IsSuccess)
            {
                _confirmingDeactivate = false;
                await Toast.Success(
                    string.Format(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture, Loc["DeactivateSuccessFormat"], _user.FullName),
                    summary: Loc["ActionSuccessTitle"]);
                await ReloadUserSilentlyAsync();
            }
            else
            {
                await Toast.Error(result.Error ?? Loc["Error_Generic"], summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
            }
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            await Toast.Error(Loc["Error_Generic"], summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
        }
        finally
        {
            // Only clear the flag when it still matches this action —
            // prevents a stale reset if a concurrent action somehow
            // interleaved (defense-in-depth against the rare race).
            if (_busyAction == actionKey) _busyAction = null;
        }
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Dispatches ActivateUserCommand. Called by the Activate button
    /// directly (no confirmation — activation is a non-destructive
    /// restore, mirrors the desktop's HandleActivateAsync and
    /// MobileAdminUsers's HandleActivateAsync). Sets _busyAction =
    /// "action:activate" (same state-machine pattern as Deactivate).
    /// On success: toast + ReloadUserSilentlyAsync so the status badge
    /// flips to "Active" without a full-page reload. On failure: toast
    /// the handler's error.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task HandleActivateAsync()
    {
        if (_busyAction is not null) return;
        if (_user is null || !CanActivate() || _user.IsActive) return;

        var actionKey = "action:activate";
        _busyAction = actionKey;

        try
        {
            var result = await MessageBus.InvokeAsync<Result>(
                new ActivateUserCommand(_user.Id));

            if (result.IsSuccess)
            {
                await Toast.Success(
                    string.Format(CultureInfo.CurrentCulture, Loc["ActivateSuccessFormat"], _user.FullName),
                    summary: Loc["ActionSuccessTitle"]);
                await ReloadUserSilentlyAsync();
            }
            else
            {
                await Toast.Error(result.Error ?? Loc["Error_Generic"], summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
            }
        }
        catch (Exception)
        {
            await Toast.Error(Loc["Error_Generic"], summary: Loc["ErrorTitle"]);
        }
        finally
        {
            if (_busyAction == actionKey) _busyAction = null;
        }
    }

    // ── Navigation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Navigates back to the mobile Admin Users list. forceLoad:false —
    /// in-circuit navigation preserves the MobileLayout shell + BottomNav
    /// state (matches MobileProductDetail's GoToProducts pattern).
    /// </summary>
    private void GoToUsers()
        => Navigation.NavigateTo("/m/Admin/Users", forceLoad: false);

    // ── Display helpers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

    /// <summary>
    /// Returns the resx KEY for a Gender enum value. The markup wraps
    /// the call in Loc[...] to look up the localized label. Same pattern
    /// as MobileSettings.razor's GetGenderLabel (returns the KEY, not
    /// the localized label — keeps the helper static + side-effect-free).
    /// </summary>
    private static string GetGenderLabel(Gender gender) => gender switch
    {
        Gender.Male => "GenderMale",
        Gender.Female => "GenderFemale",
        _ => "GenderMale"
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// Returns the localized label for a role constant. Falls back to the
    /// raw role name if no translation is defined. Same mapping as the
    /// desktop UserDetail.razor's GetRoleLabel + CreateUser.razor's role
    /// labels.
    /// </summary>
    private string GetRoleLabel(string role) => role switch
    {
        Roles.Admin => Loc["RoleAdmin"],
        Roles.Manager => Loc["RoleManager"],
        Roles.Employee => Loc["RoleEmployee"],
        Roles.ReadOnly => Loc["RoleReadOnly"],
        Roles.Customer => Loc["RoleCustomer"],
        _ => role
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// Maps a role to a CSS badge class for color coding (mobile
    /// equivalent of the desktop's GetRoleBadgeStyle — uses the
    /// .m-badge-* color tokens already established by MobileAdminUsers's
    /// list page so a role reads the SAME color on the list and the
    /// detail page). Admin → danger (highest privilege — visually
    /// distinct), Manager → warning, Employee → info, ReadOnly →
    /// neutral, Customer → success (buyer role — positive connotation).
    /// </summary>
    private static string GetRoleBadgeClass(string role) => role switch
    {
        Roles.Admin => "m-badge-danger",
        Roles.Manager => "m-badge-warning",
        Roles.Employee => "m-badge-info",
        Roles.ReadOnly => "m-badge-neutral",
        Roles.Customer => "m-badge-success",
        _ => "m-badge-neutral"
    };
}
